<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aristodemus of Cumae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Aristodemus Malakos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aristodemus, called Malakos, was tyrant of Cumae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> in the late 6th and early 5th centuries BCE. After defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aricia">Aricia</a> around 504 BCE, he seized power at Cumae with popular support, gave refuge to the exiled Roman king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquinius Superbus</a>, and was later overthrown and killed by the sons of the aristocrats he had driven out.</p></div>
